#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""FlashProve adapter (RFC-0005), standard library only.

Usage: entrypoint <info|setup|commit|prove|verify|score> /io/job.json /io/out.json

Each step calls one upstream program the way upstream's own scripts call it at the pin (U below
is https://github.com/anthropics/FlashProve/blob/9e2bdd8ff5bf9c62f18263d7dac189b0400b260c):

- `commit`: `cp_commit -weights W -config C -M 1 -ivk IVK -isk ISK`, as U/guide/performance.md
  "Reproduce the numbers" line 2 (one leaf table, the seed drawn by cp_commit, so masking is on as
  in the timed runs). W must hold the five weight files with the fit's `wsums.txt` and `fold.txt`
  beside them, and C the fit's `policy.txt`, `KL.bin` and `layout.txt` (U/examples/maverick/
  run.sh, stage_fit and stage_commit). The ID comes from the line `commitment ID: ` and success
  from the last line `cp_commit: done. ... is complete and can be handed out`
  (U/docs/operators-duties.md step 10). The secret key directory, whose files share names, is
  handed to the harness as one tar file; the verifier gets the config files and the public key.
- `prove`: one session of `cp_prove -isk ISK -spool S -batch B -in-step K -most-batches N -memory
  lean,tensor-rooms=8` with HOME and TMPDIR below the work directory, every request laid into
  `in/` before the start: what U/driver/measure_rate.py does (lines 567-571 and 685; K is 6, or B
  if B is smaller). A request is T token ids, then r and e, then a 32-byte nonce, under the name
  sha256("p258-spool-req-1" || bytes) (U/docs/service.md "A request file"). cp_prove releases a
  batch's responses into `out/` together; a response is complete once `<id>.proof` exists
  (U/docs/service.md "A response"). Each is hard-linked into /io/proofs/.tmp-req-<i>/ and
  released by rename as soon as it is seen.
  With one output token (the v0.1 workloads) the prompt fills the window, r = T - 1 and e = T, and
  the proof states the next token. With a longer reply (Level 1, RFC-0018 AD-1, AD-3) prove first
  has the committed integer model write each reply, greedily, with the package that upstream ships
  for it (U/integrations/vllm/integer_model/README.md "Python API" and "A request from the model's
  own reply"), after that package's float64 check on the GPU (its OPERATING.md "Check that float64
  products are exact on your GPU"); the request is then prompt, reply and filler 0 up to T, with
  r = P - 1 and e = P + R - 1 (U/docs/verifying.md lines 115-128).
- `verify`: `cp_verify CONFIG IVK REQUEST TOKEN PROOF -id ID [-next-token] -j N`, as
  U/driver/measure_rate.py line 698 and U/docs/verifying.md line 176; `-next-token` only when the
  request asks only about y (U/verifier/docs/output.md line 314). The request and token files are
  written here from the job (the client's side, U/docs/verifying.md), never taken from the proof
  directory. The verdict map is RFC-0005 §Verdict normalisation over U/verifier/docs/output.md
  "Exit codes".

The programs need an x86-64 CPU with the SHA extensions and, for cp_commit and cp_prove, a
sandbox that lets madvise(MADV_WIPEONFORK) through (U/prover/docs/build.md "What you need").

- `score` (RFC-0018 AD-7): the integer-model package's greedy choice at every position of a reply
  it is given, and optionally the largest logits there, on `setup`'s fit. The package is the one
  `prove` writes the replies with; upstream says it is "by design" the function cp_prove proves and
  does not state that the two are equal value by value at full size (README.md §Scoring a reply).

IPROOFS_IO and IPROOFS_MODELS map /io and /models to host directories, and IPROOFS_FLASHPROVE_SRC
names the upstream checkout (default /src), when the tests run this file in process.
"""

